.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
library owns discovery and lifecycle policy above
.Xr libchannel 3 .
It does not define application payload schemas.
Service-specific libraries remain the normal application interface and use
.Nm
internally.
.Pp
The primary discovery class is the global service:
.Bl -tag -width "Global service"
.It Global service
A manifest-declared name is reserved by
.Xr serviced 8 ,
may activate its provider on demand, and yields a new direct channel session
for each successful
.Fn service_connect .
The supervisor is not a data-plane proxy.
.El
.Pp
A bundle may also reach a private helper unit of its own through
.Fn service_helper_open ,
which is resolved bundle-locally and is not part of the global namespace.
The service-specific library fixes the discovery class and wire protocol.
.Sh SHARED CONTEXT
.Fn service_acquire
validates and consumes the sealed serviced bootstrap once per process.
Initialization is thread-safe and idempotent.
Independent typed libraries receive logical references to the same context.
A terminal bootstrap error is stable for the process and is returned to every
later acquisition attempt.
.Pp
.Fn service_release
releases a logical reference.
The process context intentionally remains alive while sessions, listeners, or
the supervisor dispatcher may still use it.
An inherited context is invalid in the child of
.Xr fork 2 ;
fresh discovery attempts fail with
.Er ECHILD .
.Pp
.Fn service_authorize_capabilities
activates the bootstrap tokens assigned to a managed process.
.Fn service_enter_capability_mode
starts lifecycle dispatch and explicitly enters Capsicum capability mode.
.Fn service_ready
never changes security state and fails with
.Er EPERM
unless capability mode has already been entered.
These context operations are used by managed processes that are not global
providers.
.Pp
.Fn service_idle_shutdown
requests that
.Nm serviced
stop this provider after
.Fa seconds
of no new client demand; a subsequent lookup relaunches it on demand.
A provider calls it when it goes idle and calls it again after handling a
client to re-arm the timer, or with
.Fa seconds
set to zero to cancel a pending idle shutdown.
New client demand arriving before the timer fires cancels it automatically.
.Pp
.Fn service_supervisor_fd
returns a borrowed event descriptor which becomes readable after permanent
supervisor loss.
.Fn service_supervisor_status
returns the stable transport status without consuming that event.
The private control channel read by
.Nm
is not public.
.Pp
Capabilities are acquired on demand, by name, at runtime; no capability is
minted or delivered at launch.
Each of the following client entry points resolves the relevant broker over the
process channel and drives it; the broker scopes the request by the caller's
unforgeable channel label.
Each returns 0 on success, or
.Va -1
with
.Va errno
set.
.Pp
.Fn service_storage_open
asks the
.Dq system.Filesystem
provider
.Pq Xr tzfsd 8
for the owned, type-checked storage claim named
.Fa name ,
mounts the delivered rights-limited handle, and returns its directory root in
.Fa dirfdp .
The call retains the handle for the life of the process, because the anonymous
mount is anchored by it \(em dropping the handle would force-unmount the
directory.
The caller hardens the returned directory's rights itself.
.Pp
.Fn service_storage_open_quota
is
.Fn service_storage_open
with the persistent claim's
.Fa refquota
bounded to
.Fa quota
bytes;
.Fa quota
of zero selects the daemon's configured default, and a value below the daemon's
floor is rejected with
.Er EINVAL .
.Fn service_storage_open
is
.Fn service_storage_open_quota
with
.Fa quota
of zero.
.Pp
.Fn service_storage_destroy
reclaims and destroys the persistent storage claim previously granted under
.Fa name ,
freeing its pool space.
The claim is resolved under the caller's own label-scoped namespace, so a
caller can never name another's;
.Er ENOENT
is returned when the named claim is absent.
.Pp
.Fn service_storage_list
enumerates the caller's own persistent and cache storage claims into
.Fa claims ,
storing the number written in
.Fa countp .
The walk is scoped to the caller's own label-derived namespace, so it can only
ever return the caller's claims.
Enumeration is paged: pass
.Fa cursorp
pointing at zero for the first page; on return
.Fa *cursorp
is nonzero when more claims remain (re-issue with that value) and zero after the
last page.
A buffer of
.Dv SERVICE_STORAGE_LIST_MAX
entries always holds a whole page; a smaller
.Fa max
that cannot hold the returned page fails with
.Er EMSGSIZE
rather than truncating silently.
Each returned
.Vt struct service_storage_claim
carries the claim's opaque
.Va name ,
its
.Va used
bytes, and its
.Va refquota
ceiling in bytes
.Pq zero when none .
.Pp
.Fn service_open_config
opens this service's writable, label-scoped configuration area \(em the
well-known
.Dq config
claim under its per-service home \(em and returns its mounted directory root in
.Fa dirfdp .
It is a thin wrapper over
.Fn service_storage_open
and takes no object name.
.Pp
.Fn service_open_isolated
opens an existing filesystem
.Fa path
through
.Xr tzfsd 8 Ns 's
per-label default-deny policy and returns a Capsicum-rights-limited,
close-on-exec descriptor in
.Fa fdp .
It lets a sandboxed service reach a device node, shared directory, or config
file it cannot open by path itself, with nothing declared in the manifest.
.Fa rights
is a mask of
.Dv SERVICE_OPEN_READ , SERVICE_OPEN_WRITE , SERVICE_OPEN_EXEC ,
.Dv SERVICE_OPEN_LOOKUP ,
and
.Dv SERVICE_OPEN_IOCTL ;
a non-zero
.Fa is_dir
requires that
.Fa path
be a directory.
A path the label is not granted fails with
.Er EACCES .
.Pp
.Fn service_capability_open
opens an owned, type-checked descriptor declared by the manifest under
.Fa name
with interface
.Fa type ,
returning it in
.Fa fd .
.Pp
.Fn service_enter_namespace
asks the
.Dq system.Namespace
provider
.Pq Xr warden 8
to create a jail rooted at
.Fa path
(scoped by this process's channel label) and attaches the calling process to it;
the returned jail's root credential authorizes the attach, so a non-root caller
may confine itself.
.Fa hostname
and
.Fa ip4_addr
may be
.Dv NULL .
.Fa flags
is zero for a persistent jail (reused by label across restarts) or
.Dv SERVICE_NS_EPHEMERAL
for a jail whose lifetime is bound to the calling process.
.Pp
.Fn service_enter_namespace_ex
is
.Fn service_enter_namespace
with an additional IPv6 address
.Fa ip6_addr
.Pq empty or Dv NULL for none
and the full
.Dv SERVICE_NS_*
flag set, including
.Dv SERVICE_NS_VNET
for a jail with its own virtual network stack (requires
.Dv VIMAGE ) .
.Fn service_enter_namespace
is this with
.Fa ip6_addr
empty.
.Pp
.Fn service_destroy_namespace
destroys the caller's own jail, scoped to the caller's label so it can never
name another's;
.Er ENOENT
is returned when the caller has none.
.Pp
.Fn service_namespace_info
reports the caller's own jail into
.Fa out .
A label owns at most one jail:
.Va out->present
is 1 with the fields filled, or 0 when the caller has no jail (which is still a
success).
.Va out->flags
carries the
.Dv SERVICE_NS_*
bits describing the jail \(em
.Dv SERVICE_NS_VNET
when it has its own virtual network stack, and
.Dv SERVICE_NS_EPHEMERAL
when this process's own connection is anchoring the jail's lifetime \(em so a
consumer that lists after a restart can pass them straight back to
.Fn service_enter_namespace_ex .
Each address field is empty when the jail has no address of that family.
.Pp
.Fn service_ensure_extension
asks the
.Dq system.SystemExtension
provider
.Pq Xr sysextd 8
to ensure the kernel extension (module) named
.Fa module
is loaded (success includes an already-loaded module).
.Fa module
must be a single, safe filename component.
This is a
.Dv SYSTEM Ns -domain
caller only; the discovery domain layer makes the name unresolvable for user
services, so it fails closed for them.
.Pp
.Fn service_extension_stat
queries whether the kernel extension named
.Fa module
is loaded without attempting a load, setting
.Fa *loadedp
to 1 (loaded) or 0 (not).
It is routed and validated exactly like
.Fn service_ensure_extension ;
a denied name fails with
.Er EPERM .
.Pp
.Fn service_vsock_listen
asks the
.Dq system.VM
provider
.Pq Xr waspnest 8
to bind a host-local listening
.Dv AF_VSOCK
socket on the caller's behalf.
.Fa port
is an index within the caller's own label-scoped port window
.Pq Li 0 .. VMD_PORTS_PER_LABEL-1 ,
so it can never name another component's port;
.Fa backlog
is the listen backlog (0 = default).
On success
.Fa fdp
receives the close-on-exec listening descriptor to
.Xr accept 2
on, and
.Fa cidp
and
.Fa portp ,
when non-NULL, the concrete host-local CID and port bound.
.Pp
.Fn service_vsock_connect
dials a peer's advertised vsock via
.Xr waspnest 8
and returns the connected
.Dv AF_VSOCK
socket in
.Fa fdp .
.Fa port
is the concrete port the peer advertised from its own
.Fn service_vsock_listen
reply (not a window index), and
.Fa cid
is the target CID, which must not be
.Dv VMADDR_CID_ANY .
.Pp
.Fn service_helper_open
launches and connects a private helper unit declared in the caller's own
bundle, named by its bare unit
.Fa name
.Pq the helper unit's directory name, not a
.Ql system.
name .
The service manager resolves it through a synthetic bundle-local provider name
that ordinary
.Fn service_connect
lookups cannot reach, activates the helper on demand when it is not already
running, and returns a new direct channel session in
.Fa session_fd .
The helper joins the caller's coalition and so shares the caller's lifetime.
A name the bundle does not declare as a helper fails with
.Er ENOENT ;
the delivered endpoint is transfer-confined like any other session.
Returns 0 on success, or
.Va -1
with
.Va errno
set.
.Pp
.Fn service_activation_socket
returns the manager-owned socket-activation listener delivered under the logical
.Fa name ,
or
.Va -1
with
.Va errno
set to
.Er ENOENT
when the process was launched with no such listener
.Po
.Er EINVAL
for a malformed name
.Pc .
A socket-activation source is declared in the unit manifest as
.Li activation { socket = { name = ...; listen = ...; } } :
.Nm serviced
itself binds and
.Xr listen 2 Ns s
the socket and holds it open, so it survives this provider's crashes,
idle stops, and restarts with its backlog intact
.Pq a queued connection is never dropped .
The first inbound connection is the demand that launches the unit.
Unlike the on-demand broker calls above,
this call returns the retained descriptor itself, not a duplicate:
it is owned by libservice and remains valid for the life of the process, so the
caller
.Xr accept 2 Ns s
or receives on it but must
.Em not
close it.
.Sh GLOBAL PROVIDERS
.Fn service_provider_create
creates the provider lifecycle object and acquires the shared context.
Each call to
.Fn service_provider_expose
creates an independent listener and synchronously claims one
manifest-authorized name with the supervisor.
A process may expose multiple names and route each accepted session through
its corresponding listener.
An undeclared name fails with
.Er EACCES ,
and duplicate local or supervisor claims are rejected.
.Pp
.Fn service_provider_expose_sendable
is identical except that it declares the name's delivered sessions
.Em sendable :
the service manager leaves each consumer's endpoint transfer-unlimited so the
consumer may re-send it
.Pq attenuating per hop with Xr cap_xfer_limit 2 ,
rather than the default of attenuating it so it cannot be forwarded.
Whether a service's sessions may propagate is the provider's own protocol
contract, so it is declared here in the provider's code, not in the manifest.
.Pp
The manifest reserves names before launch; exposure checks in the corresponding
listener without making the name connectable.
When client demand activates a name,
.Nm
runs its activation callback and reports success or failure for that name
without forcing the provider's other names to initialize.
Queued clients are released only after successful per-name activation.
.Pp
.Fn service_provider_enter_capability_mode
starts lifecycle dispatch and explicitly enters Capsicum capability mode if
necessary.
.Fn service_provider_ready
never changes security state.
It fails with
.Er EPERM
unless capability mode has already been entered, and then reports process
readiness.
It also fails unless every name in the manifest's
.Va provides
array has been claimed.
.Xr serviced 8
independently verifies the process-descriptor capability-mode event.
.Pp
.Fn service_listener_accept
returns zero separately from the owned session descriptor.
The identity structure contains authenticated destination-name and client
label information supplied by the supervisor.
.Fn service_listener_close
withdraws the claim, wakes blocked accepts, closes queued sessions, and
consumes the listener.
Existing direct sessions are unaffected.
New lookups remain queued against the manifest reservation until the provider
claims a replacement listener.
.Pp
.Fn service_provider_worker_channel
asks the supervisor to create an unnamed capability-channel pair for a
provider and one internal worker.
It must be called before capability-mode entry.
On success both returned descriptors are owned by the caller,
non-transferable, and locked against exec propagation.
The provider endpoint is forced closed in a child; the worker endpoint may
cross exactly one fork boundary and is then forced closed by any later fork
in both processes.
The pair is never entered in the global service namespace.
Providers use
.Xr libchannel 3
or a managed session on these endpoints; libservice assigns no payload schema.
.Sh WORKER PROTECTION
After a provider forks a per-request worker,
.Fn service_worker_protect
activates its assigned process-protection policy without requiring an inherited
context object.
.Fn service_worker_drop_inherited_authority
then discards every private descriptor retained by libservice before the
worker enters capability mode.
.Sh MANAGED SESSIONS
.Fn service_session_create
consumes
.Fa fd
on success and creates a thread-safe blocking convenience adapter over the
asynchronous
.Xr libchannel 3
transport.
It does not duplicate the transport's framing or correlation token.
.Pp
.Vt struct service_message
contains a versioned payload and borrowed descriptor array.
.Vt struct service_reply
separates buffer and descriptor capacities from returned lengths and includes
authenticated sender metadata.
.Vt struct service_call_options
sets a bounded timeout.
.Pp
Concurrent calls share one dispatcher and are correlated by channel reply
tokens.
Reply reordering cannot deliver one call's response to another.
A timed-out or canceled call discards its late reply and closes all attached
descriptors.
Peer death completes every outstanding call.
.Pp
When a typed library detects a malformed service-protocol reply,
.Fn service_session_fail
atomically marks the session terminal with a positive
.Xr errno 2
value.
The first terminal error wins, concurrent waiters are awakened, and every
later call returns that error without sending another message.
This lets typed protocol code fail closed without racing session destruction;
transport framing and concurrency remain owned by
.Nm .
.Pp
On
.Er EMSGSIZE ,
the reply reports the required payload length and descriptor count; received
descriptors remain library-owned and are closed.
.Fn service_session_close
cancels active waiters, closes queued event descriptors, and consumes the
session.
.Sh DESCRIPTOR OWNERSHIP
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Fn service_connect ,
.Fn service_listener_accept ,
and both outputs from
.Fn service_provider_worker_channel
return owned descriptors through output parameters.
.It
.Fn service_session_create
consumes its descriptor only on success.
.It
Outgoing message descriptors are borrowed for the call.
.It
Received attachment slots remain owned by their message or bootstrap object
until explicitly taken.
.It
Supervisor, label, and capability accessors return borrowed process-context
state.
.El
.Sh SECURITY
Bootstrap descriptors are injected only from a validated serviced bootstrap.
Their environment locators and original injected descriptors are consumed
when acquired.
An unmanaged process cannot create authority by setting the deployment
variable itself.
.Pp
.Ev CAPABILITY_UNIT_DIR
names the selected unit directory so a managed program can locate immutable
configuration and resources in either a base-system or versioned site bundle.
It is location metadata, not an authority descriptor; programs must open
needed files before entering capability mode or use a declared filesystem
descriptor.
.Pp
Service names authorize discovery, not application operations.
Typed protocols must validate magic, version, opcode, status, payload shape,
attachment count, and every attachment slot before use.
Delivery durability and remote cancellation remain service-specific.
